iec62351 — third-party attribution
====================================================================

This module's own code is MIT (see the repository LICENSE). It additionally
reproduces third-party CAPTURED WIRE DATA (raw GOOSE frame bytes, not source
code) whose copyright notice must be preserved in any copy. Redistributing
this module — alone or as part of zig-libs — must keep this file with it.

This adds no condition beyond MIT's own: it is the same "preserve the notice"
requirement that already applies to zig-libs' own code, owed to a different
author. No third-party SOURCE CODE is present — only three raw Ethernet
frames (IEC 61850-8-1 GOOSE publications captured from real substation IEDs),
reproduced as byte-literal test fixtures in `src/goose_capture_test.zig`.

## cutaway-security/goosestalker — real GOOSE capture frames

Vendored from https://github.com/cutaway-security/goosestalker, pinned at
commit `c68185dc6a9e4c9e28b6e14b499b1c2c251c5e49` (`main` branch,
2021-12-15):

  * `PCAPs/GOOSE.pcap`, frame 1 of 8 — a GE (General Electric) F650
    protection relay's GOOSE publication (`GEDeviceF650/LLN0$GO$gcb01`,
    `APPID` 0x0001, no VLAN tag in the source capture). The other seven
    frames are the same publisher's unchanged heartbeat retransmissions
    (`stNum`/`sqNum` differ only), so one is vendored, not all eight.
  * `PCAPs/Sample_File_GOOSE.pcap`, frame 1 of 451 — a mock four-bus/18-IED
    substation's `AA1C1Q01A1LD0/LLN0$GO$gcb_A` GOOSE publication (`APPID`
    0x3001). The source capture carries an IEEE 802.1Q VLAN tag between the
    MAC addresses and the GOOSE EtherType; the 4 VLAN octets are stripped
    before the frame is embedded, matching `goose.zig`'s documented contract
    that the caller resolves the MAC domain (VLAN included) before calling
    `parse` — see that file's module doc comment. The remaining 450 frames in
    this capture are the same 18 IEDs' repeated heartbeats of the same
    shapes, already represented by the one frame vendored (and by the
    `Sample_File_MMS_and_GOOSE.pcap` frame below, a different IED in the same
    family).
  * `PCAPs/Sample_File_MMS_and_GOOSE.pcap`, frame 39 of 301 (the first GOOSE
    frame in a capture that interleaves GOOSE with MMS/TCP traffic) — the
    sibling IED `AA1C1Q05A1LD0/LLN0$GO$gcb_A` (`APPID` 0x3001), VLAN-stripped
    as above.

Excluded from vendoring, deliberately:

  * `PCAPs/GOOSE_wireshark.pcap` — byte-identical to `GOOSE.pcap` (same 8
    frames), a duplicate kept upstream for a different tool's default file
    name. Vendoring it would add nothing.
  * `PCAPs/GOOSE_DEMO.pcap` — a 1-frame synthetic demo GOOSE (`goID`s
    `"111"`/`"222"`/`"333"`, plainly placeholder data, not a real device
    capture) bundled in the same repository. Excluded as redundant with the
    three genuine device captures above.
  * The remaining ~484 frames across `Sample_File_GOOSE.pcap` and
    `Sample_File_MMS_and_GOOSE.pcap` — repeated heartbeats of the same
    publishers already represented; every frame surveyed (493 total, across
    all five `.pcap` files in the source repository) has `Reserved 1` =
    `Reserved 2` = `0x0000`, i.e. **no IEC 62351-6 security extension in any
    of them** — see `SPEC.md`'s "Real GOOSE captures" section for what that
    means for this module's interoperability claim.

